<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Bit Agusi</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 740 BCE (approx.) · capital Arpad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bit Agusi, the Aramaean kingdom of Arpad, a leader of north Syrian resistance to Assyria, known from the Sefire treaties.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusi">Gusi</a>, an Aramaean chief, founded the kingdom in the early 9th century BCE in the plains north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. It took the name House of Gusi, and its capital became Arpad, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-rifaat">Tell Rifaat</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attar-shumki-i">Attarshumki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matiilu">Mati'ilu</a>, led coalitions of Syrian states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mat-river">Mati</a>'ilu signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nirari-v">Ashur-nirari V</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> and another, preserved on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sefire-steles">Sefire steles</a>, with Bar-Ga'yah of KTK, giving rare insight into Aramaean treaty language and curses.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matiilu">Mati'ilu</a> joined an alliance led by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu</a>. Tiglath-Pileser III besieged Arpad from 743 to 740 BCE, captured it and made Bit Agusi an Assyrian province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bit-agusi.jpg" alt="Bit Agusi" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Hans van Deukeren (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NeoHittiteStates.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 740 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arpad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Attarshumki, Mati'ilu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Aramaean religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arpad, Kingdom of Arpad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-820&amp;focus=bit-agusi" aria-label="Bit Agusi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5613 862 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bit Agusi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5933 1118L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5933 1118h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5933 1118h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arpad. <a href="/atlas?year=-820&amp;focus=bit-agusi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

